**Solution Outline**

We are given a tree with \(n\) vertices, each vertex \(i\) has a population \(r_i\).  
The band travels along a simple path (never repeating a vertex) and may give concerts in some of the cities it visits.  
The concert sequence must be strictly increasing by population along the direction of travel.  
The band wants to maximise the number of concerts. Hence we need the longest strictly increasing subsequence (LIS) that can be obtained on some simple path, where the path can be traversed in either direction.

---

**Key observations**

1. **Direction matters** – For an undirected path \(\{u,v\}\) the sequence of vertices from \(u\) to \(v\) is the reverse of the sequence from \(v\) to \(u\).  
   An increasing subsequence in one direction is a decreasing subsequence in the opposite direction.  
   Therefore the best we can obtain on the undirected path is  
   \[
   \max\bigl(\text{LIS}(u\to v),\; \text{LDS}(u\to v)\bigr).
   \]

2. **Extension to leaves** – If an endpoint of an optimal path is not a leaf, we can extend the path from that endpoint into a neighbouring vertex that is not on the path. The original path is a suffix/prefix of the longer path, so any increasing subsequence of the original remains a valid subsequence of the longer one. Consequently we can always extend the path until both ends are leaves without decreasing the answer. Hence it is sufficient to consider only paths that start at a leaf.

3. **LIS / LDS on a moving path** – For a fixed start vertex \(s\) we can run a DFS and maintain the current path from \(s\) to the current node \(v\).  
   * `inc` – the standard patience‑sorting array for LIS of the current sequence.  
     `inc[k]` = minimal possible last value of an increasing subsequence of length \(k+1\).  
     To append a new value \(x\) we replace the first element \(\ge x\) (or append if none).  
   * `dec` – the same thing for LDS. Because LDS of the original sequence is LIS of the negated sequence, we work with the values \(-x\).  
     `dec[k]` = minimal possible last **negated** value of a decreasing subsequence of length \(k+1\).  

   The lengths of these arrays are exactly the LIS and LDS lengths of the current path.  
   After processing a child we roll back the changes, so the same two vectors can be reused for the next leaf start.

4. **Answer** – The global answer is the maximum of (inc length, dec length) over all visited nodes and all leaf starts.

---

**Complexity**

* Number of leaves \(L\) can be up to \(n\) (e.g. a star).  
* For each leaf we traverse the whole tree (\(n\) vertices).  
* Each vertex visit performs two binary searches on vectors whose length is at most the depth of the current path (≤ \(n\)).  
* Total: \(O(L\cdot n\log n)\) which is at most \(O(n^2\log n)\). With \(n\le 6000\) this is perfectly acceptable in C++.

---

**C++ Implementation**

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

const int MAXN = 6005;
int n;
int r[MAXN];
vector<int> adj[MAXN];
int ans = 1;

void dfs(int u, int p, vector<int>& inc, vector<int>& dec) {
    int x = r[u];
    int neg = -x;

    // ---- update inc (LIS) ----
    int inc_pos = -1, inc_old = 0;
    auto it = lower_bound(inc.begin(), inc.end(), x);
    if (it == inc.end()) {
        inc.push_back(x);
    } else {
        inc_pos = it - inc.begin();
        inc_old = *it;
        *it = x;
    }

    // ---- update dec (LDS) ----
    int dec_pos = -1, dec_old = 0;
    auto it2 = lower_bound(dec.begin(), dec.end(), neg);
    if (it2 == dec.end()) {
        dec.push_back(neg);
    } else {
        dec_pos = it2 - dec.begin();
        dec_old = *it2;
        *it2 = neg;
    }

    // update global answer
    ans = max(ans, (int)inc.size());
    ans = max(ans, (int)dec.size());

    // recurse
    for (int v : adj[u])
        if (v != p)
            dfs(v, u, inc, dec);

    // rollback
    if (inc_pos == -1)
        inc.pop_back();
    else
        inc[inc_pos] = inc_old;

    if (dec_pos == -1)
        dec.pop_back();
    else
        dec[dec_pos] = dec_old;
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    cin >> n;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) cin >> r[i];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        int a, b;
        cin >> a >> b;
        adj[a].push_back(b);
        adj[b].push_back(a);
    }

    vector<int> inc, dec;
    inc.reserve(n);
    dec.reserve(n);

    // start from every leaf
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        if (adj[i].size() == 1) {   // leaf
            dfs(i, 0, inc, dec);
        }
    }

    cout << ans << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

